#! ia 1.0

@convention authoring-concepts
  meaning
    says "What IA is. IA is a language and toolchain for records: text files headed #! ia 1.0 that the CLI admits or refuses. Records describe. A contract, law or mandate states what must hold, and enforces nothing until an evaluator runs; admission means the records conform, not that they are true. Word: a registered record kind (@spec, @workspace, @contract and so on); one word, one owning system. System: owns words (schemas, consent for edges, direct requires) and one accountable steward; create one only for missing stable semantics or native release ownership, not for every repository or team. Steward: a record, the @agent a @system names, with a stewardship @mandate whose authority covers the system folder; CLI 3.4.0 renders no steward agent and registers no steward guard. Workspace: composes systems and authored sources for a purpose; owns no vocabulary; authorised participants author instances of adopted words in it; grants no filesystem or tenant access and does not authenticate a user. Workspace-system: owns the workspace and distribution words; it is a vocabulary owner, distinct from a particular workspace instance or a hosted workspace database. Distribution and kit: a distribution ships a selected native record closure (IA's content-addressed archive and lock, a different artifact from a host plugin ZIP; complete selected system folders can contain private methods, and selection is not redaction); a kit is an adoption profile, not a word, reusable selected definitions packaged and adopted by a consumer, and it supplies no authority the consumer never granted; a kit profile of one distribution rooted on one workspace is a selected profile, not a universal rule. Agent and mandate: a participant and its declared moves, scope and covered paths; declarations, not grants; since 3.0 the CLI consults them only to gate its own commands (--actor), and an authored @hook's guard judges the acting participant's Synthesis move, not covers (3.3.0 and 3.4.0); a steward pointer is not a running model or an automatic invocation. Contract, check, case: falsifiable requirements, the check that judges them, and success and failure cases whose evaluators name real tests; not-evaluated is not pass. Plugin: skills, references, scripts and role briefs; not a native system or distribution, and a plugin package need not match one. Organization, tenant, department: product identity, isolation and access concepts; a department may be a scoped view, a tenant key must bind trusted context, and none requires a new IA word just for labeling. The IA model in full is the authoring-ia-model-* conventions."
    answers "What are IA's concepts, and what does each one do and not do?"
  governance
    severity advisory
    requires "Use each concept only for what this table says it does, and never describe a declaration as a grant, a check or a running agent."
  subject
    subject-word system

@convention authoring-public-systems
  meaning
    says "Eleven public systems own the 28 domain words, plus two floor words (system, schema) and fourteen taxonomy words that describe the closed coordinates (44 words in the 3.1.1, 3.2.2, 3.3.0 and 3.4.0 catalogues). agent-system owns agent and mandate: define participants, bounded moves and scopes and policy-delivery requirements. agent-composition-system owns voice, agent-profile, harness, capability and execution-binding: compose roles, bounded capabilities, host contracts and installed execution paths. authoring-system owns authoring-guide and operation: teach a word and define concrete callable operation inputs, outputs and effects. compliance-system owns contract, check and case: state falsifiable promises, success and refusal cases and executable evidence. governance-system owns law, principle, convention and playbook: supply concise guidance and phase and primitive procedure cells. hook-authoring-system owns hook: describe event, tool or path guard intent without claiming automatic registration. learning-system owns observation and improvement: retain revision-bound observations, limitations and reviewed improvement proposals. session-system owns run: describe governed execution identity and state without confusing it with a launched process. template-system owns template: draft parameterized records and assets with bounded rendering. work-system owns plan, milestone, task, decision and spec: track dependencies, source-bound evidence and accepted design decisions. workspace-system owns workspace and distribution: compose systems and sources and describe native release closures. These are eleven reusable language mechanisms, not eleven plugins that each need to load for every request: load only the owners and references relevant to the task. Use the public language catalogue and the exact owning schema as authority (ia vocabulary <word> --schema --json); do not hard-code a complete cloned catalogue as another registration authority. Adding a discriminator does not extend the closed taxonomy axes; a document genre, team name, plugin package or output format need not become a discriminator. A workspace's local systems sit in .ia/src/systems/<system>/ (system.ia, schemas/, records/, contracts/, checks/, cases/, README.md, AUTHORING.md); a folder may author a word only if its system directly requires the word's owner, and an edge needs consent from both owners (rules R1 and R2)."
    answers "Which public system owns which IA word, and when is each one used?"
  governance
    severity advisory
    requires "Author each IA word only from a folder whose system directly requires that word's owner, and ground the word's shape in the exact owning schema, never in a remembered catalogue."
  subject
    subject-word system

@convention authoring-toolchain
  meaning
    says "The toolchain and the resolver rule. Target: @inventarch/cli 3.5.0, language inventarch/language 3.2.0, grammar #! ia 1.0. Package semver, language distribution, grammar header, plugin version and host version are different coordinates. Since 3.0, ia preview shows what one record, field or relationship would compile to and ia author writes it, but ia author refuses @workspace, @agent, @mandate, @system, @schema, @agent-profile and @hook changes; ia compile is gone (use ia capture); and read, next, validate, preview, author, capture and project are gated by the acting participant's @mandate (--actor, by default the workspace's steward or its one participant), while ia position is not. Never run the ia on the PATH; it may be an older release. Not npx --no ia: with no workspace install it runs a globally installed CLI, often an older one. In this kit the IA CLI runs through the core's scripts/ia.mjs, which resolves IA_CLI_PATH, else the repository's pinned @inventarch/cli (searched up to the repository root, never above it), else exactly the core's pinned @inventarch/cli (3.4.0) through npx, never an ia on PATH and never a shell; a gate that needs the CLI takes it from the core's checker runner as ${IA}. Pin the workspace's own install with npm install --save-dev --save-exact @inventarch/cli@3.5.0. The kit's probe and governance lint capture a scratch copy through the core's helper (scripts/lib/scratch.mjs), which resolves the CLI the same way, takes --cli <@inventarch/cli/dist/main.js> first and IA_CLI_NO_NPX=1 (or IA_CORE_NO_NPX=1) to forbid npx, refuses a CLI below 3.x and prints ia <version> from <source> on stderr; npx runs exactly 3.4.0. The MCP door: IA ships a read-only MCP server; ia host claude --apply (or codex, cursor) registers it on this machine as the ia-workspace server (tools mcp__ia-workspace__*, among them ia_scope, ia_vocabulary, ia_search, ia_context, ia_resolve and ia_read); the files it writes are machine-local and never committed. It serves only the repository it was registered for, so reads of any other root, and every read without it, use the CLI; nothing depends on it; validation, capture and every write still use the CLI. A host ia host does not cover can add it by hand with the repository's IA version and an absolute root: command npx (npx.cmd on Windows), args -y -p @inventarch/mcp-door@<version> ia-mcp-door --root <absolute repository path>; in a governed repository do not install @inventarch/mcp-door into node_modules, because verify framework refuses an unpinned @inventarch package. Do not install, restore, fetch, activate, create organisations or edit access just to answer an authoring question."
    answers "Which IA CLI and language does this kit target, how is the CLI resolved, and how is the read-only MCP door used?"
  governance
    severity advisory
    requires "Run IA only through the core's ia.mjs, IA_CLI_PATH or the checker runner's ${IA}, at the pinned 3.5.0, and use the door only for reads of the one repository it serves."
  subject
    subject-word system

@convention authoring-current-authoring
  meaning
    says "Current workspace-first authoring. 1, Inspect the selected root, installed language pins, current source bindings and schemas; distinguish tool version, language distribution, grammar header and artifact format. 2, Use a workspace when existing words suffice; author instances under its declared authored root; create a local system only for reusable definition or native release ownership. 3, Declare an agent participant and mandate when policy delivery needs them; current fields are authority.participant, moves, scope, covers and excluded-words; they do not grant host access or guarantee CLI enforcement. 4, Use stable names for new instances and preserve original adopted identities; resolve every relationship and typed field target in the intended closure. 5, Draft only admitted sections and fields from the canonical schema; current workspace composition.sources and steward are not accepted by every retained legacy schema. 6, Validate in scratch with unchanged pins and source bytes; apply only exact authorised draft changes to live source, then run the selected checks; report not-evaluated and unavailable evidence explicitly. 7, Acceptance, runtime qualification, publication and native release packaging each need their own task authority and evidence; they do not follow from structural validation. The minimal current example (a @workspace project with meaning, composition systems [agent-system, workspace-system, work-system], sources .ia/src @authored and steward @agent project-participant; that @agent with governance applies []; and a @mandate project-mandate whose governance requires Claim no host permission from this declaration and whose authority names participant, moves [Observation, Verification, Synthesis], scope [@workspace project] and covers [.ia/src/]) is an instructional shape, not a universal preset or deployed guard: select narrower policy as the task requires and validate exact target compatibility."
    answers "How are instances of existing words authored in a workspace today?"
  governance
    severity advisory
    requires "Author workspace instances in these seven steps, validating in scratch before applying exact authorised changes."
  subject
    subject-word spec

@convention authoring-what-goes-where
  meaning
    says "What goes where. Language, CLI, runtime and distribution primitives: the IA project (the released @inventarch/cli 3.5.0 and its language distribution). A system's records: .ia/src/systems/<system>/ (system.ia, schemas/, records/), written by its steward inside the folder its stewardship mandate covers. Instances of adopted words (specs, plans, milestones, tasks, decisions, contracts, cases and the rest): the workspace's authored root, as its composition.sources declares (.ia/src @authored), written by an authorised participant; no new system is needed for them. The workspace's own participant and mandate: .ia/src/ beside the @workspace. Bindings of the core's entry points to kit capabilities: .ia/bindings.json, written through the core's bind.mjs or kit.mjs. Captured snapshots and evidence: .ia/work/ (snapshot/current.json from ia capture, evidence/<revision>.ia from ia validate), generated and never hand-edited; the locked language and installed kits: .ia/distributions.lock.json and .ia/distributions/, written by ia restore and the kit tools. Accepted organisation knowledge (policies, cross-team PRDs, decisions, plans): the organisation's knowledge base, the Knowledge lane's concern; implementation-coupled designs, tests and generated references stay in the owning source repository, which a knowledge anchor may point at. Probe and capture copies: temporary directories the scripts create and delete, outside the workspace and outside the IA home; never the real workspace. A host plugin's files: the plugin's own folder, and a kit workspace lowered from it a separate folder; a plugin catalogue's house rules: its validator and schemas. Installed kits: the repository's .ia/distributions.lock.json installs the kits it runs (a packages row or a package entry, at any version); the core's config/kits/ and <IA home>/kits/<kit>/<version>/ hold copies available to install. This kit: reusable method only, no organisation corpus, tenant data, review-private bodies or access grants. When work crosses an ownership boundary, link to the owner's source rather than copying its corpus."
    answers "Where does each kind of IA material live?"
  governance
    severity advisory
    requires "Put each material in the home this table names, and link to an owner's source instead of copying its corpus across a boundary."
  subject
    subject-word system

@convention authoring-how-to-author
  meaning
    says "How to author. 1, Orient: run ia position for your seat with the pinned CLI; if it refuses, run the command it names; it takes shape, phase, seat and word, never free text. 2, Check the scope first (authoring-scope): records of existing words need only a workspace and an authorised participant; a new word, system, workspace or kit is out of this kit's scope, to be authored by hand from the public IA documentation or with an InventArch subscription. A repository, plugin, department or service is not automatically a system. 3, Read the schema: ia vocabulary <word> --schema --json (no workspace needed), or the local schemas/<word>.schema.ia for a word the workspace mints; re-seat at the owning system for a word this workspace does not own; a steward pointer is readable, not an invocation. 4, Probe before you write, in a scratch copy (authoring-probing-the-ia-toolchain); never learn a rule by breaking the real workspace. 5, Write within your authority: a steward in its covered folder; a participant in its declared scope. Validate with ia validate --severity error --json and judge the findings, not the exit code. Check governance clauses, which IA admits under any name (the governance lint, rule R11). 6, Report honestly: declared, structurally admitted, executed and accepted are different. Ground every claim (path:line, or inferred or unknown); never write enforced, verified or passes without the run; never promote a stage or close a gate as a side effect; check the blast radius of renames; author and reviewer are different roles; product and authority choices go to the person with options. Return what is verified, what is provisional, what is still broken and the next bounded step. Never expose private review bodies or tenant data. For a concrete question, identify the source owner, consumer workspace, selected IA and runtime version and intended reader, then explain the smallest existing vocabulary that expresses the need; cite the exact source and keep apart implemented source, published release, recorded decision and proposed design. Full steps: the current-authoring convention (authoring-current-authoring)."
    answers "What are the steps for authoring IA records, from orientation to the report?"
  governance
    severity advisory
    requires "Author in these six steps, in order, and report the evidence state of every claim."
  subject
    subject-word system
